# .spool/spec.req

feature: SPEC

requirements:

  - id: SPEC-1
    title: Interface Field
    description: >
      A requirement may name its interface stub with `interface`: a path, or
      a list of paths, relative to the project root. Spool reports an active
      or draft requirement whose interface path doesn't exist.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-11-07
    rationale: >
      The pipeline approves an interface stub before any tests are written,
      and the agents are pointed at it. A spec naming a stub that was moved
      or never written sends them to the wrong place, so it fails like an
      untested AC. A list, because a stub often spans several files.
    superseded_by:
    out_of_scope:
      - Requiring every requirement to have an interface
      - Globs in interface paths
      - Checking that a path stays inside the project root
      - Checking what the stub contains
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: SPEC-1-1
        title: Interface Parsed
        description: >
          Given a requirement whose interface is a single path or a list of
          paths,
          when its .req file is parsed,
          then the requirement holds those paths in the order given, a
          single path as a list of one, and a requirement with no
          interface, an empty list or a null one holds none.

      - id: SPEC-1-2
        title: Malformed Interface
        description: >
          Given a requirement whose interface is a map, a list holding a
          map or a list, or an empty path,
          when its .req file is parsed,
          then the file fails to parse with an error naming the
          requirement's ID and the interface field.

      - id: SPEC-1-3
        title: Missing Interface Reported
        description: >
          Given a requirement whose status (SPEC-4-4) is active or draft
          and whose interface names a path that exists as neither a file
          nor a directory under the project root,
          when spool runs,
          then the report lists one finding per missing path, giving the
          requirement's ID and the path as written: the text report under
          a "Missing Interfaces" heading as `<ID>: <path>`, the markdown
          report under a heading of the same name, and the JSON report as
          "missingInterfaces", a list of {"id", "path"} that is [] when
          there are none. Findings are ordered as the report's features, then
          as the requirements are in the .req file, then as the paths are
          written. With no findings, the text and markdown reports have no
          such heading. Paths that exist,
          and deprecated or superseded requirements, give no finding.

      - id: SPEC-1-4
        title: Missing Interface Severity
        description: >
          Given .spool.yaml's severity.missing_interface set to error,
          warning, info or off, or not set,
          when spool runs with a missing interface path,
          then it exits 1 only when the severity is error, which is the
          default, and the finding is reported whatever the severity.

      - id: SPEC-1-5
        title: Missing Interface Diagnostic
        description: >
          Given an active or draft requirement (SPEC-4-4) with a missing
          interface path,
          when the language server publishes diagnostics,
          then the .req file has one warning per missing path on the
          requirement's ID line, naming that path. Deprecated and superseded
          requirements get none.

  - id: SPEC-2
    title: Out of Scope Field
    description: >
      A requirement may list what it leaves out with `out_of_scope`. Spool
      parses it and shows it in the JSON report and the requirement's
      hover, so tools that hand a requirement to an agent can pass it on.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-11-07
    rationale: >
      Specs already use out_of_scope to stop the test and implementation
      agents from building more than was asked for, but Spool drops it, so
      a tool reading Spool's output can't pass it on. The JSON report had
      no per-requirement entries to carry it, so it gains them, along with
      the interface paths.
    superseded_by:
    out_of_scope:
      - Showing out_of_scope in the text or markdown report
      - Adding out_of_scope to the --ids output (COVERAGE-2-6 fixes its keys)
      - Linting what out_of_scope says
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: SPEC-2-1
        title: Out of Scope Parsed
        description: >
          Given a requirement whose out_of_scope is a single item or a list
          of items,
          when its .req file is parsed,
          then the requirement holds those items in the order given, a
          single item as a list of one, and a requirement with no
          out_of_scope, an empty list or a null one holds none. Given an out_of_scope
          that is a map or a list holding a map or a list, the file fails
          to parse with an error naming the requirement's ID and the
          out_of_scope field.

      - id: SPEC-2-2
        title: Requirements in the JSON Report
        description: >
          Given --format json,
          when spool runs,
          then the report has "requirements", a list with one object per
          requirement the report counts (SPEC-4-6) holding exactly "id",
          "feature", "title", "status" (SPEC-4-4), "interface" (the paths
          as written) and "outOfScope", each list
          [] rather than null when empty, ordered as the report's features,
          then as in the .req file. The report's other keys are unchanged.

      - id: SPEC-2-3
        title: Out of Scope in the Hover
        description: >
          Given a requirement with out_of_scope items,
          when the language server shows the hover for the requirement's ID,
          then the hover shows what it shows today, then an "Out of scope"
          line followed by one bullet per item in order. Given a
          requirement with none, when its hover is shown, then it is
          unchanged.

  - id: SPEC-3
    title: Given/When/Then Lint
    description: >
      Spool reports an active or draft acceptance criterion whose
      description isn't written as Given/When/Then.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-11-07
    rationale: >
      The test agent derives tests from each AC's Given, When and Then, so
      an AC that skips one leaves the agent to guess. The check is loose on
      wording and line breaks and strict on order. Invariants hold on every
      run rather than describing one, so they are not written this way and
      are not checked.
    superseded_by:
    out_of_scope:
      - Checking invariants, requirement descriptions or titles
      - Checking what each clause says
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: SPEC-3-1
        title: Given/When/Then Form
        description: >
          Given an AC description,
          when it is checked,
          then it passes when, ignoring leading whitespace, it starts with
          the word "given", and the words "when" and "then" follow later in
          that order, matching whole words in any case across line breaks.
          "Given a project, when spool runs, then it exits 0" passes, and
          so does "GIVEN x WHEN y THEN z" with each clause on its own line.
          "Given x, then y", "When x, given y,
          then z", "Givens x, when y, then z", "Given x, then y, when z"
          and "Given x when y" fail.

      - id: SPEC-3-2
        title: Form Findings Reported
        description: >
          Given ACs whose status (SPEC-4-5) is active or draft and that
          fail the check,
          when spool runs,
          then the report lists one finding per such AC, giving its ID and
          its feature: the text report under a "Not Given/When/Then"
          heading as `<ID>: <title>`, the markdown report under a heading
          of the same name, and the JSON report as "notGivenWhenThen", a
          list of {"id", "feature"} that is [] when there are none.
          Findings are ordered as the report's features, then as the
          requirements are in the .req file, then as the ACs are written.
          With no findings, the text and markdown reports have no such
          heading. Deprecated ACs, ACs of superseded
          requirements, and invariants give no finding.

      - id: SPEC-3-3
        title: Form Severity
        description: >
          Given .spool.yaml's severity.given_when_then set to error,
          warning, info or off, or not set,
          when spool runs with an AC that fails the check,
          then it exits 1 only when the severity is error, which is the
          default, and the finding is reported whatever the severity.

      - id: SPEC-3-4
        title: Form Diagnostic
        description: >
          Given an active or draft AC (SPEC-4-5) that fails the check,
          when the language server publishes diagnostics,
          then the .req file has a warning on the AC's ID line saying it
          isn't written as Given/When/Then. Deprecated ACs, ACs of
          superseded requirements, and invariants get none.

      - id: SPEC-3-5
        title: No Issues Line
        description: >
          Given a text report with a missing interface or an AC that fails
          the check, and no other findings,
          when spool runs,
          then the report doesn't end with "Full traceability — no issues
          found."

    invariants:

      - id: SPEC-3-INV-1
        title: Lint Leaves Coverage Alone
        description: >
          Whatever interfaces are missing and whatever ACs fail the check,
          the coverage counts in the report, and the output and exit status
          of spool --ids, are what they would be without those findings.

  - id: SPEC-4
    title: Item Status
    description: >
      Each AC and invariant may have its own status: draft, active or
      deprecated. Coverage counts an item only when its status is active, and
      a requirement's status is folded from its items' statuses.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-11-09
    rationale: >
      Approving a spec means approving its items, and a revision often adds
      ACs to a requirement that already has passing tests. With one status
      per requirement, the new ACs either fail coverage before anyone has
      approved them or hide the requirement's existing coverage while they
      wait. With a status per item, approval is setting an item to active,
      and only approved items are expected to have tests. An item with no
      status takes its requirement's, so existing specs keep their meaning.
      The folded status puts draft above active, so a requirement with
      anything awaiting approval shows as draft; it decides what the lint
      checks and what tools see, not what coverage counts. Drafts are
      linted so a malformed AC or a missing stub is caught in review, before
      approval. Superseding retires a whole requirement, so it stays a
      requirement-level status. An unknown status fails the parse, so a
      typo can't quietly drop an item from coverage.
    superseded_by:
    out_of_scope:
      - Showing item status in the hover, the code lenses, or the text and
        markdown reports
      - Item status in the api package's views
      - Changing spool --ids for a listed ID that is draft or deprecated
      - Superseding a single AC or invariant
      - Tooling to approve items; approving is editing their status
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: SPEC-4-1
        title: Item Status Parsed
        description: >
          Given an AC or invariant whose status is draft, active or
          deprecated, or that has no status,
          when its .req file is parsed,
          then the item's status is the one written, or its requirement's
          status as written when it has none.

      - id: SPEC-4-2
        title: Unknown Status
        description: >
          Given a requirement whose status is not draft, active, deprecated
          or superseded, or an AC or invariant whose status is not draft,
          active or deprecated,
          when its .req file is parsed,
          then the file fails to parse with an error naming that ID and the
          status as written.

      - id: SPEC-4-3
        title: Missing Requirement Status
        description: >
          Given a requirement with no status that has no ACs or invariants,
          or has one with no status,
          when its .req file is parsed,
          then the file fails to parse with an error naming the requirement's
          ID and the status field. A requirement with no status whose ACs and
          invariants all have one parses.

      - id: SPEC-4-4
        title: Folded Requirement Status
        description: >
          Given a requirement,
          when its .req file is parsed,
          then its status is superseded when it is written as superseded;
          otherwise, when it has ACs or invariants, the highest of their
          statuses (SPEC-4-1) in the order deprecated < active < draft;
          otherwise the status written. This is the requirement's status
          wherever Spool shows it or filters on it: the language server's
          traceability tree, the api package's views and Status filter, and
          the JSON report's "requirements".

      - id: SPEC-4-5
        title: Coverage by Item
        description: >
          Given requirements with active, draft and deprecated ACs and
          invariants,
          when spool runs,
          then an item is active when its status (SPEC-4-1) is active and its
          requirement isn't written as superseded, and only active items are
          counted, listed as untested, warned about as untested by the
          language server, or affect the exit status. Draft and deprecated
          items, and every item of a superseded requirement, are none of
          these, whatever their tests.

      - id: SPEC-4-6
        title: Requirements Counted
        description: >
          Given requirements with and without active ACs or invariants
          (SPEC-4-5),
          when spool runs,
          then a requirement is counted in its feature's and the report's
          requirement totals when at least one of its ACs or invariants is
          active, or when it has none and its status is active; every other
          requirement is counted as excluded.

      - id: SPEC-4-7
        title: Item Status in the Tree
        description: >
          Given the language server's traceability tree,
          when it is built,
          then each AC and invariant in it has "status", its status as in
          SPEC-4-1, and each requirement's "status" is its folded status
          (SPEC-4-4).

    invariants:

      - id: SPEC-4-INV-1
        title: Requirement Status Alone Is Unchanged
        description: >
          In a project where no AC or invariant has a status of its own, an
          item is active exactly when its requirement's status is active,
          and every requirement's status is the one written.

  - id: SPEC-5
    title: Spec Hash
    description: >
      `spool hash <ID>,<ID>,...` prints a hash of the listed ACs and
      invariants as the agents see them, so a tool can tell whether the
      spec changed after the tests for those items were locked.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-11-11
    rationale: >
      The slice verifier must fail when a slice's spec changes after its
      tests were locked, or tests written for one spec pass against
      another. The hash covers what the test agent works from: each
      item's ID, title and description, and its requirement's ID, title,
      description, interface and out_of_scope. Rationale, deciders, dates
      and status are left out: approving or deprecating an item says
      whether it counts, not what it requires, and coverage checks react
      to that already. Whitespace inside a field is collapsed so that
      reflowing a paragraph doesn't break a lock. The canonical form is
      written out in full because hashes are stored and compared across
      Spool versions; changing it would break every lock, so it is
      changed only by a new requirement. Each field is length-prefixed so
      no two different specs share a canonical form.
    superseded_by:
    out_of_scope:
      - Expanding a requirement ID to its ACs and invariants
      - Hashing tests, and recording the hash (slice's lock step)
      - Reading the IDs from a file or from stdin
      - A JSON format, or the hash in spool --ids output
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: SPEC-5-1
        title: Hash Printed
        description: >
          Given spool hash with a comma-separated list of AC and invariant
          IDs that .req files define,
          when it runs,
          then stdout holds the hash as 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits
          and a newline, and nothing else, and it exits 0, whatever the
          coverage of those items and whatever the configured severities.

      - id: SPEC-5-2
        title: Canonical Form
        description: >
          Given listed IDs,
          when the hash is computed,
          then it is the SHA-256 of the canonical form: one record per
          distinct listed item, ordered by normalized ID in byte order.
          A record is these fields, in order: the item's ID, title and
          description; its requirement's ID, title and description; the
          number of interface paths, then each path as written; the number
          of out_of_scope items, then each item as written. IDs are
          normalized; every other text has each run of whitespace
          replaced by one space and leading and trailing whitespace
          removed; numbers are in decimal. Each field is written as its
          length in bytes in decimal, a colon, the field's UTF-8 bytes,
          and a newline.

      - id: SPEC-5-3
        title: Covered Fields Change the Hash
        description: >
          Given listed IDs and their hash,
          when any field of SPEC-5-2 changes other than in whitespace,
          including adding or removing an interface path or out_of_scope
          item, or the list gains or loses an item,
          then the hash changes.

      - id: SPEC-5-4
        title: Unknown IDs
        description: >
          Given spool hash with a well-formed AC or invariant ID that no
          .req file defines,
          when it runs,
          then stderr names each such ID, stdout is empty, and it exits 1.

      - id: SPEC-5-5
        title: Bad Arguments
        description: >
          Given spool hash with no ID list, more than one argument, an
          empty list, an empty item, a malformed ID, or a requirement ID,
          when it runs,
          then it prints a message to stderr naming the bad item as it
          was given (any message will do for a missing or empty list or
          item), prints nothing to stdout, and exits 2.

    invariants:

      - id: SPEC-5-INV-1
        title: Hash Ignores What It Doesn't Cover
        description: >
          Reordering, repeating, or changing the case or leading zeros of
          the listed IDs; changing whitespace inside a field; changing
          status, rationale, deciders, consulted, date or superseded_by;
          editing other items or requirements; and moving a requirement
          to another .req file of its feature never change the hash.
